  "result": "I found the bug and wrote a plan, but I haven't changed any code. Plan mode is on, and the exit-plan tool isn't available here, so I can't request approval formally. Tell me to go ahead and I'll make the change.\n\n**Cause:** `IntRange._clamp` in `src/click/types.py:754` returns `bound - dir`. `convert` passes `dir` as +1 when clamping up to the minimum and -1 when clamping down to the maximum. The subtraction therefore moves away from the range when a bound is open. For example, `IntRange(min=0, min_open=True, clamp=True)` given `-5` returns `-1`, but it should return `1`.\n\n**Fix:** change that line to `return bound + dir`. `FloatRange` isn't affected, because it rejects `clamp` with open bounds.\n\n**Tests:** I'd add `IntRange` cases to `tests/test_types.py`. They'd cover an open minimum (\u22125 \u2192 1), an open maximum (50 \u2192 9 with `max=10`), and closed bounds still clamping to the bound itself. Then I'd run `pytest` on the types and info-dict tests, and after that the full suite.\n\nThe plan is saved at `<home>\\.claude\\plans\\some-users-report-that-effervescent-horizon.md`.",
